A||N||9||AusFiero||Finally a new option on an engine swap.||||14||
Z||000000||AusFiero||06-07-2001||11:20 AM||||Well in this part of the world it isn't cheap to repair Fiero engines or put any of the common American upgrades in them.<P>So I decided to start searching around and found a very compact all alloy V8 which comes in a range of sizes from 3.5 ltr to 5.0 ltr. Can be normally aspirated, fuel injected or even twin turboes from the factory. Plus it can be hooked up to a gearbox that is available here cheaply and be centermounted in the engine cradle rather than offset to the right as the engine is now. Of all engines to find it is a Rover V8. <BR>The setup I just describe I discovered today is also lighter than a standard Fiero V6 and gearbox setup and I could do the swap for about $3000 or my prefered option about $6000 with a twin turboed version. Or about $3000 US<P>This setup is also physically smaller than a V6 Fiero setup.<P>I will let the pics do the rest of the talking. <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rover2_1.jpg"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rover1.jpg"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rover4.jpg"> <BR>The Gearbox is relocatable. Setup for a Leyland mini in this case. <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rover6.jpg"> <BR>I wasn't joking.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/fiero5.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A><BR>Need a Subwoofer Console or Speaker Door Pods?||||reg||14
Z||000001||Slammed Fiero||06-07-2001||12:14 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1607505&a=12259132&p=44997589&Sequence=0&res=high"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1607505&a=12259132&p=44995512&Sequence=0&res=high"> <P>The motor was designed and built in the early 60's (62??) by oldsmobile and buick. The designer of the motor is Bill Mitchell. The rights to the motor were sold in 67 to British Leyland. The olds version was available in a Turbo as well. The engine weighs 320lbs complete. Shares some architechture with the Buick RWD 3.8. The one above is mounted in our 66 Corvair Corsa , it's 4.3ltrs , forged pistons , balanced blueprinted, everything has been done. The motor pictured above runs a Marine Grind camshaft from Lunati since the Motor sits behind the transmission. The only problem in north america is spped parts for the motor are very expensive. The motor is still used in the range rover , and was used in the TR8 and MG GT V8 (Both I think were imported to the US. The Buick motor used a little different heads than the olds motor , but the olds motor is physically stronger.<P>The starter is on the wrong side.. I had this idea about 5 years ago as I have 3 spare motors in the garage. One has a buick 300 crank and has been bored and stroked to 5.0ltrs ...weighs in at 326lbs ..Complete!!<P><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1607505&a=12259132&p=48230089&Sequence=0&res=high">     <BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals..Autocrossed , Tracked , abused.||||reg||1
Z||000002||AusFiero||06-07-2001||12:26 PM||||Not sure on the full specs of the Aussie version but at $300 for a rebuilder and reasonably cheap speed parts available it is certainly looking to be an option. <P>Starter motor will not concern me as I wouldn't be mating it to a Fiero transaxle. Fit shouldnt be a problem in any way. If it fits in a Mini it will fit in a Fiero easily. Most of the weight and size saving will come from ditching the Fiero transaxle and using a combination of other Leyland parts. <p>[This message has been edited by AusFiero (edited 06-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Raydar||06-07-2001||01:12 PM||||A V8 in a Mini?! My GAWD!!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"||||reg||1
Z||000004||Philphine||06-07-2001||05:00 PM||||can't remember the exact issue, i think it's mar. 86, but hot rod mag did an article on these engines and gave some parts sourses and people who dealt with them (don't know how good that would be now), and a parts interchange chart for a build up of up to 305 ci. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Wade87GT||06-07-2001||07:15 PM||||That engine was the Hot Rodder's motor of choice in England throughout the 70's and 80's. They put it in everything you could imagine. About the TR-8, the U.S. was the only market with about 2500 built in 1980 and 1981 (except for 12 RHD cars for England). I've had my TR-8 since '82 and that engine is really nice in a lightweight car. It did pretty well for the Range Rover too in the Paris to Dakar races.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Pontiaddict||06-07-2001||07:33 PM||||TVR uses those motors and their 5 liter gets 300+ HP naturally asperated. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Archie||06-10-2001||06:30 PM||||Interesting pics of the Rover engine and that transaxle.<P>However, I have a couple of questions.<P>Did you notice that the engine is on the wrong side of the axle shafts? (or you have the pics. flipped 180 degrees) <P>Are you intending to install the engine with the teansaxle over on the right side of the engine compartment?<P>Also, have you noticed that the transaxle out put shafts are a lot lower in comparison to the crankshaft centerline than they are on a Fiero Transaxle? This would have the engine sitting MUCH higher in the engine compartment than when using a Fiero transaxle.<P>You can compare the above features by comparing your pics. to this pic.<P>Also, the distance from the cranskshaft CLine to the Axle CLine appears to be several inches greater than the Fiero trasaxle.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Mvc-314s_1.jpg"> <P>I hope this helps.<P>BTW, don't forget that other deal we were talking about.<P>Archie<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||KRMFiero||06-10-2001||07:36 PM||||How about one of them Honda engines? them things scream 5-8K Redline that would be nice even though it is a import engine<P>How about Honda/Acura's 3.2L V-6 Vtec  i bet if someone could make a kit for that (or even the regular 4 cyl vtec) would be fun in a fiero<P>Kyle||||reg||1
Z||000009||AusFiero||06-10-2001||09:02 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Archie:<BR><B>Interesting pics of the Rover engine and that transaxle.<P>However, I have a couple of questions.<P>Did you notice that the engine is on the wrong side of the axle shafts? (or you have the pics. flipped 180 degrees) <P>Are you intending to install the engine with the teansaxle over on the right side of the engine compartment?<P>Also, have you noticed that the transaxle out put shafts are a lot lower in comparison to the crankshaft centerline than they are on a Fiero Transaxle? This would have the engine sitting MUCH higher in the engine compartment than when using a Fiero transaxle.<P>You can compare the above features by comparing your pics. to this pic.<P>Also, the distance from the cranskshaft CLine to the Axle CLine appears to be several inches greater than the Fiero trasaxle.<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Mvc-314s_1.jpg">  <P>I hope this helps.<P>BTW, don't forget that other deal we were talking about.<P>Archie<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Still only in the head scratching stage at the moment Archie  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Yes I noticed the transaxle was all wrong in that particular picture. But figure if they can get one in a Mini it must be possible. Well change must to may.<P>Have also been considering the Jap V8s as they are really cheap here. <P>Main consideration I am trying to work with really is weight and handling characteristics. How many KGs does a Chev swap add to a Fiero Archie? An LT1 would be nice but they are to expensive here.<P>I am actually trying to get away from having an ECM anyway so if I went Chev V8 I would have it naturally aspirated rather than fuel injected. <P>When I am ready, if I bought one of your kits would you allow another forum member to ship it to me. If it arrives as a "gift"  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> it avoids hefty import duties.<P>With your V8 conversions how have you found the standard Fiero brakes to be? My car does not stop well at all and every single bit of the braking system has been replaced or renewed, excluding the steel lines and the proportional valve, which "experts" tell me is workig fine. How they know that by looking at a proportional valve I don't know. The factory recall kit on the rear calipers was the first thing I did.<P>Anybody got a cheap Proportional valve lieing around ? Can't buy one new and I really don't think it is a part than can be deemed working by just looking at it and blowing through it as the experts did.<P>I haven't forgotten our talks Archie. I am getting the small stuff out of the way first to generate the income to finish the other. Will be in touch. <BR><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/fiero5.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A><BR>Need a Subwoofer Console or Speaker Door Pods?||||reg||1
